WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.025
[Instructor] - Now let's talk about solution

2
00:00:01.025 --> 00:00:03.582
architecture on AWS.

3
00:00:03.582 --> 00:00:05.580
At a very high level, because there are so many

4
00:00:05.580 --> 00:00:09.420
combinations, but it all looks a little bit similar if you

5
00:00:09.420 --> 00:00:10.800
look at it from a high level.

6
00:00:10.800 --> 00:00:13.590
So we have our users, and they need to get access

7
00:00:13.590 --> 00:00:16.750
to our application, so the first thing they do is talk

8
00:00:16.750 --> 00:00:17.800
to a DNS.

9
00:00:17.800 --> 00:00:21.660
And the DNS layer is usually going to be your route 53.

10
00:00:21.660 --> 00:00:24.770
Then they have two possible routes from there.

11
00:00:24.770 --> 00:00:27.600
They have to find some static content and some

12
00:00:27.600 --> 00:00:28.990
dynamic content.

13
00:00:28.990 --> 00:00:31.830
Usually the static content is going to be spread all around

14
00:00:31.830 --> 00:00:34.450
the world, it's going to be cached by having a CDN

15
00:00:34.450 --> 00:00:37.769
layer, a content delivery network, and usually that

16
00:00:37.769 --> 00:00:39.220
is going to be CloudFront.

17
00:00:39.220 --> 00:00:42.870
And CloudFront is going to source this data, usually

18
00:00:44.395 --> 00:00:47.548
in a static form from S3 or Glacier, but we all know that

19
00:00:47.548 --> 00:00:50.360
CDN of CloudFront can also get it from custom sources

20
00:00:50.360 --> 00:00:52.900
and we'll learn all these topics one by one.

21
00:00:52.900 --> 00:00:55.320
What I want to expose to you right now is all the

22
00:00:55.320 --> 00:00:57.390
plus switches we have and the kind of architectures

23
00:00:57.390 --> 00:01:00.180
we have to get into and the choices we have to make.

24
00:01:00.180 --> 00:01:01.013
Okay.

25
00:01:01.013 --> 00:01:04.070
So, on a static asset layer surge, we have S3, which is

26
00:01:04.070 --> 00:01:07.310
going to be perfect for CloudFront, or Glacier for

27
00:01:07.310 --> 00:01:09.900
archives and it doesn't work directly with CloudFront.

28
00:01:09.900 --> 00:01:12.850
Okay, on the web layer now, which is the more dynamic

29
00:01:12.850 --> 00:01:16.580
content, we have to set up a load balancer or a gateway.

30
00:01:16.580 --> 00:01:19.330
So we have classic load balancer, application load

31
00:01:19.330 --> 00:01:22.580
balancer, network load balancer, API gateway, or even

32
00:01:22.580 --> 00:01:26.030
an elastic IP, if we don't need that amount of layer,

33
00:01:26.030 --> 00:01:28.120
for example if you have a very simple solution in the

34
00:01:28.120 --> 00:01:29.390
backup.

35
00:01:29.390 --> 00:01:33.090
Then that web layer has to talk to a compute side,

36
00:01:33.090 --> 00:01:37.060
and that compute side may be something like EC2, or

37
00:01:37.060 --> 00:01:42.060
an auto scaling group, AWS Lambda, ECS, Fargate, Batch or

38
00:01:43.030 --> 00:01:46.610
EMR, and obviously you cannot connect EMR with a web layer

39
00:01:46.610 --> 00:01:48.940
but what I wanna show you is that if there's no web layer,

40
00:01:48.940 --> 00:01:50.980
then we can still have a compute layer that does some

41
00:01:50.980 --> 00:01:52.140
batch computations.

42
00:01:52.140 --> 00:01:54.150
It's really hard to represent all the solutions

43
00:01:54.150 --> 00:01:56.610
architecture and AWS into one slide, but I want to be able

44
00:01:56.610 --> 00:01:59.020
to divide to you the different categories so you get

45
00:01:59.020 --> 00:02:01.080
a better understanding of all the kind of things we're

46
00:02:01.080 --> 00:02:03.699
going to do a deep dive on in the future lectures.

47
00:02:03.699 --> 00:02:04.920
Okay.

48
00:02:04.920 --> 00:02:08.080
By the way, the platform layer can also be in front of

49
00:02:08.080 --> 00:02:09.896
your web layer, as you can see in the arrow from the

50
00:02:09.896 --> 00:02:12.550
CDN to the web layer so it is possible to get

51
00:02:12.550 --> 00:02:14.330
different directions as well.

52
00:02:14.330 --> 00:02:18.030
Okay, next your data needs to be somewhere so usually

53
00:02:18.030 --> 00:02:20.380
you have a database, and that database can have multiple

54
00:02:20.380 --> 00:02:24.990
forms, it can be RDS, Aurora, DynamoDB, ElasticSearch,

55
00:02:24.990 --> 00:02:28.080
S3 is considered a database as well, or Redshift if you have

56
00:02:28.080 --> 00:02:30.360
more analytical type of workloads.

57
00:02:30.360 --> 00:02:32.635
It's also very possible that you will need a caching layer,

58
00:02:32.635 --> 00:02:36.465
and so you can cache stuff or it can have a section layer

59
00:02:36.465 --> 00:02:39.220
for cashing your section data, so it could be Elastic cache,

60
00:02:39.220 --> 00:02:43.220
DAX if you have infinite of DynamoDB, DynamoDB itself can

61
00:02:43.220 --> 00:02:47.350
also be a session layer, and RDS, it's very possible to put

62
00:02:47.350 --> 00:02:50.940
your sessions in RDS, even though ElactiCache is probably

63
00:02:50.940 --> 00:02:54.330
going to be a little bit more scalable and faster.

64
00:02:54.330 --> 00:02:57.470
Okay, next there's possible that your instances will need

65
00:02:57.470 --> 00:03:01.607
to fetch static data as well, so this data can be on EBS,

66
00:03:01.607 --> 00:03:03.740
EFS or directly on the instance stores.

67
00:03:03.740 --> 00:03:05.810
Again, it depends on the kind of requirements you are going

68
00:03:05.810 --> 00:03:08.915
to have for your solution architecture and you need to be

69
00:03:08.915 --> 00:03:12.100
able to decouple your solutions if you have a micro services

70
00:03:12.100 --> 00:03:13.620
type of architecture.

71
00:03:13.620 --> 00:03:17.800
And so it could be using SQS, SNS, Kinesis where they have

72
00:03:17.800 --> 00:03:20.600
streams of data, Amazon MQ, Step Functions

73
00:03:20.600 --> 00:03:23.390
for orchestration, and so on.

74
00:03:23.390 --> 00:03:25.790
Finally, it is possible that your compute layer also needs

75
00:03:25.790 --> 00:03:28.555
to be accessing some static assets, so S3 Englisher

76
00:03:28.555 --> 00:03:30.560
is a definite option.

77
00:03:30.560 --> 00:03:33.700
So these are all some kind of combinations, and if you

78
00:03:33.700 --> 00:03:36.770
try to permit all of them, you can get really an infinite

79
00:03:36.770 --> 00:03:39.250
number of solution architecture, but what I want you

80
00:03:39.250 --> 00:03:41.660
to realize at this high level is that as we get into

81
00:03:41.660 --> 00:03:44.500
solution architecture discussions based on what you choose

82
00:03:44.500 --> 00:03:46.320
for your database, your computer caching, your

83
00:03:46.320 --> 00:03:48.620
orchestration, your storage, your static, your web layer,

84
00:03:48.620 --> 00:03:51.980
your CDN, everything, it will have some impact into the

85
00:03:51.980 --> 00:03:54.910
cost, into the availability, the durability,

86
00:03:54.910 --> 00:03:58.600
the scalability, and so on, so it's really up to you as a

87
00:03:58.600 --> 00:04:01.770
solution architect to understand which type of technology

88
00:04:01.770 --> 00:04:04.970
is going to be the best for which moment, and this is what

89
00:04:04.970 --> 00:04:06.890
the exam will be testing you on.

90
00:04:06.890 --> 00:04:09.870
Now in this section, we're going to do a deep dive into

91
00:04:09.870 --> 00:04:12.450
everything in this slide, so don't worry, there will be

92
00:04:12.450 --> 00:04:14.680
specific solution architectures for each of these

93
00:04:14.680 --> 00:04:17.710
components, and we'll get a better discussion to which one

94
00:04:17.710 --> 00:04:20.100
is more appropriate and when and why.

95
00:04:20.100 --> 00:04:21.720
Okay, so that's it for a small introduction.

96
00:04:21.720 --> 00:04:23.270
I hope you're all excited, and I will see you

97
00:04:23.270 --> 00:04:24.270
in the next lecture.

